                %a{:href => "#zfc_and_etcs_elementary_theory_of_the_category_of_sets"} ZFC and ETCS: Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets
          %h2#zfc_and_etcs_elementary_theory_of_the_category_of_sets ZFC and ETCS: Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets
          %p
            This is a post on “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics” (eek!). I was motivated to write it while I’ve been struggling to understand better certain applications of ultrafilters – namely the theory of
            %em measurable cardinals
            – from a point of view and language that I feel comfortable with. My original intent was to blog about
            = succeed "," do
              %em that
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/07/18/ultrafilter-convergence-compactness-and-the-spectrum-of-a-boolean-algebra/"} ultrafilters
            I started in connection with the series on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/04/02/toward-stone-duality-posets-and-meets/"} Stone duality
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/06/22/basic-category-theory-i/"} category
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/06/29/basic-category-theory-ii/"} theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/07/12/basic-category-theory-iii-representability-adjoints-and-the-yoneda-lemma/"} posts
          %p
            A lot of confusion seems to reign around “the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} categorical
            approach to
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} sets
            and supplanting them with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2007/12/13/naive-set-theory-paul-halmos/"} naive
          %p
            What I
            %em do
            propose to discuss is a formalized set theory which embodies this rich tradition, but which takes advantage of categorical insights honed over the decades, and which I would argue is ‘natural’ in its ease to accept formulas in naive set theory and give them a foundation true to mathematical practice; I also argue it addresses certain criticisms which I feel could be put to that hallowed foundational theory, ZFC. I should own up that this theory is not immune to criticism, a main one being that a certain amount of preface and commentary is required to make it accessible (and I don’t think category theorists have done a particularly hot job doing that, frankly).
          %p Let’s start by putting down what we want in very simple, pragmatic terms:
          %ul
            %li A (formalized) ‘set theory’ worthy of the name ought to realize a conception of sets as “completed collections”, and allow for the existence of enough sets and relations to fulfill the needs of working mathematicians.
          %p This is intentionally vague. The “needs of working mathematicians” fluctuate over time and place and person. Some of the core needs would include the existence of the sets of natural numbers and real numbers, for instance. On the other hand, in the field of set theory one will have other needs than in, say, complex analysis. For now I’ll ignore some of the deeper needs of set theorists, and try to focus on the basic stuff you’d need to formalize what goes on in your average graduate school text (to put it vaguely, again).
          %p
            We will discuss two formalizations of set theory:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zermelo-Fraenkel_set_theory"} ZFC
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.amazon.com/Sheaves-Geometry-Logic-Universitext-Saunders/dp/3540977104"} book with Moerdijk
            %strong membership
            is handled. I’ll start with the more familiar ZFC.
          %p As everyone knows, ZFC formalizes a conception of “set” as collection extensionally determined by the members it contains, and the ZFC axioms ensure a rich supply of ways in which to construct new sets from old (pairings, unions, power sets, etc.). Considering how old and well-developed this theory is, and the plenitude of available accounts, I won’t say much here on its inner development. Instead, I want to pose a question and answer to highlight a key ZFC conception, and which we use to focus our discussion:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> Question
              \: “What are the members of sets?”
            %p
              %strong Answer: “Other sets.”
          %p
            This may seem innocent enough, but the consequences are quite far-reaching. It says that “membership” is a relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            from the collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of all “sets” to itself. (Speaking at a pre-axiomatic level, a
            %em relation
            from a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq X \times Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in \subseteq V \times V
            of pairs of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Why is this a big deal? A reasonable analogue might be
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamical_system"} dynamical systems
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are manifolds, say, then you can study the properties of a given smooth map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            and maybe say interesting things of course, but in the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a simple manifold like a 2-sphere.
          %p
            My point is that something analogous is happening in ZFC: we have a (binary) relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to itself, and we get a rich “dynamics” and feedback by iterative relational composition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            with itself, or by composing other derived binary relations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to itself. (Perhaps I should recall here, again at a pre-axiomatic level, that the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \circ R
            of a relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq X \times Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq Y \times Z
            is the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{(x, z): \exists_{y \in Y} (x, y) \in R \wedge (y, z) \in S\} \subseteq X \times Z
          %p A “behavior” then would correspond to an iterated membership chain
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x \in y) \wedge (y \in z) \wedge (z \in w) \wedge \ldots
          %p
            and there are certain constraints on behavior provided by things like the axiom of foundation (no infinitely long backward evolutions). The deep meaning of the extensionality axiom is that a “set”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is uniquely specified by the abstract structure of the tree of possible backward evolutions or behaviors starting from the “root set”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %em extremely
            complicated.
          %p
            There are other formulations of ZFC. One is by posets: given any relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq V \times V
            (never mind one satisfying the ZFC axioms), one can create a reflexive and transitive relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \leq b \text{ if and only if }  \forall_{x \in V} (x, a) \in R \Rightarrow (x, b) \in R
          %p
            The “extensionality axiom” for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            can then be formulated as the condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            also be antisymmetric, so that it is a partial ordering on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \in
            is the membership relation for a model of ZFC, then this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            is of course just the usual “subset relation” between elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Then, by adding in a suitable “singleton” operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: V \to V
            so that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi y
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \in y \text{ if and only if }  s(x) = \{x\} \leq y
          %p
            the rest of the ZFC axioms can be equivalently recast as conditions on the augmented poset structure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \leq, s)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.com/books?id=E0Xhn32xzZAC&dq=%22algebraic+set+theory%22&pg=PP1&ots=DvTjZP030_&sig=IX1kWLQ2rzsmw5gsdJ_0ow8vJEQ&hl=en&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=1&ct=result"} Algebraic Set Theory
          %p
            The structure of a ZFC poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \leq)
            is rich and interesting, of course, but in some ways a little odd or inconvenient: e.g., it has a bottom element of course (the “empty set”), but no top (which would run straight into Russell’s paradox). Categorically, there
            %em are
            some cute things to point out about this poset, usually left unsaid; for example, taking “unions” is left adjoint to taking “power sets”:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi X
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigcup F \leq X  \text{if and only if}   F \leq P X.
          %p
            In summary: ZFC is an axiomatic theory (in the language of first-order logic with equality), with one basic type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and one basic predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            of binary type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            My own reaction is that ZFC is perhaps
            %em way
            too powerful! For example, the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is an endo-relation makes possible the kind of feedback which can result in things like Russell’s paradox, if one is not careful. Even if one is free from the paradoxes, though, the point remains that ZFC pumps out not only all of mathematics, but all sorts of dross and weird by-products that are of no conceivable interest or relevance to mathematics. One might think, for example, that to understand a model of ZFC, we have to be able to understand which definable pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b)
            satisfy
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            and Cantor space?” and expect to get a well-defined answer. When you get right down to it, the idea that
            %em everything
            in mathematics (like say the number
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
          %p
            One might argue that all expressions and theorems of normal mathematics are interpretable or realizable in the single theory ZFC, and that’s really all we ever asked for – the details of the actual implementation (like, ‘what is an ordered pair?’) being generally of little genuine interest to mathematicians (which is why the mathematician in the street who says ZFC is so great usually can’t say with much specificity what ZFC is). But this would seem to demote ZFC foundations, for most mathematicians, to a security blanket – nice to know it’s there, maybe, but otherwise fairly irrelevant to their concerns. But if there really is such a disconnect between how a mathematician thinks of her materials
            %em at a fundamental level
            and how it specifically gets coded up as trees in ZFC, with such huge wads of uninteresting or irrelevant stuff in its midst, we might re-examine just how appropriate ZFC is as “foundations” of our subject, or at least ask ourselves how much of it we usefully retain and how we might eliminate the dross.
          %p We turn now to consider a categorical approach, ETCS. This will require retooling the way we think of mathematical membership. There are three respects in which “membership” or “elementhood” differs here from the way it is handled in ZFC:
          %ol
            %li “Elements” and “sets” are entities of different types. (Meaning, elements are not themselves presupposed to be sets.)
            %li When we say “element”, we never mean an object considered in isolation; we always consider it relative to the specific “set” it is considered to be a member of. (That is, strictly speaking, the same object is never thought of as “belonging to” two distinct sets – use of such language must be carefully circumscribed.)
            %li
              We consider not just “elements” in the usual sense, but what are sometimes called “generalized elements”. Civilians call them “functions”. Thus, an element of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              over a domain of variation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is fancy terminology for a function
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: U \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: 1 \to X
              corresponds to an ordinary element of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Each of these corresponds to some aspect of normal practice, but taken together they are sufficiently different in how they treat “membership” that they might need some getting used to. The first corresponds to a decision to treat elements of a “set” like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as ‘urelements’: they are not considered to have elements themselves and are not considered as having any internal structure; they are just atoms. What counts in a mathematical structure then is not what the constituents are ‘like’ themselves, but only how they are interrelated among themselves qua the structure they are considered being part of.
          %p
            This brings us right to the second point. It corresponds e.g. to a decision never to consider a number like ‘3’ in isolation or as some Platonic essence, but always with respect to an ambient system to which it is bound, as in ‘3
            %em qua
            natural number’, ‘3
            %em qua
            rational number’, etc. It is a firm resolve to always honor context-dependence. Naturally, we
            %em can
            in a sense transport ‘3’ from one context to another via a specified function like
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Q}
          %p
            My own sense is that it may be this second precept which takes the most getting used to – it certainly gives the lie to sometimes-heard accusations that categorical set theory is just a “
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.nyu.edu/pipermail/fom/1998-January/000994.html"} slavish translation
            of ZFC into categorical terms”. Clearly, we are witnessing here
            %em radical
            departure from how membership is treated in ZFC. Such unbending commitment to the principle of context-dependence might even be felt to be overkill, a perhaps pedantic exercise in austerity or purity, or that it robs us of some freedom in how we want to manipulate sets. A few quick answers: no, we don’t lose any essential freedoms. Yes, the
            %em formal
            language may seem
            %em slightly
            awkward or stilted at certain points, but the bridges between the naive and formal are mercifully fairly obvious and easily navigated. Lastly, by treating membership not as a global endo-relation on sets, but as local and relative, we effectively eliminate all the extraneous dreck and driftwood which one rightly ignores when examining the mathematics of ZFC.
          %p
            The third precept is familiar from the way category theorists and logicians have used generalized elements to extend set-theoretic notation, e.g., to chase diagrams in abelian categories, or to describe sheaf semantics of intuitionistic set theory, or to flesh out the Curry-Howard isomorphism. It is a technical move in some sense, but one which is easy to grow accustomed to, and very convenient. In ETCS, there is a strong “extensionality principle” (technically, the requirement that the terminal object is a generator) which guarantees enough “ordinary” elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: 1 \to X
            to make any distinctions that can sensibly be made, but experience with topos theory suggests that for many applications, it is often convenient to drop or significantly modify that principle. If anything in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            is a nod to traditional set theory, it is such a strong extensionality principle. [The Yoneda principle, which deals with generalized elements, is also an extensionality principle: it says that a set is determined uniquely (to within uniquely specified isomorphism) by its generalized elements.]
          %p
            Okay, it is probably time to lay out the axioms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: a \to b
            as functions or equivalently as “elements of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            over a domain of variation
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            with codomain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            (under the strictures imposed by precepts 1-3 above). For those who speak the lingo, the axioms below are those of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> Remark
              \: As ETCS breaks the “dynamical” aspects of ZFC, and additionally treats issues of membership in a perhaps unaccustomed manner, its axioms do take longer to state. This should come as no surprise. Actually, we’ve discussed some of them already in
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/06/29/basic-category-theory-ii/"} other posts on category theory
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of products
            \. For any two sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            and functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1: c \to a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2: c \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in a, y \in b
            over the same domain, there exists a unique element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle \in c
            over that domain for which
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1\langle x, y \rangle = x \qquad p_2\langle x, y \rangle = y
          %p
            A choice of product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is usually denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \times b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \times b :=_i \{\langle x, y \rangle: x \in a, y \in b\}
          %p
            where the funny equality sign and bracketing notation on the right simply mean that the cartesian product is uniquely defined up to isomorphism by its collection of (generalized) elements, which correspond to pairs of elements, in accordance with the Yoneda principle as explained in the discussion
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/06/29/basic-category-theory-ii/"} here
          %p
            We also assume the existence of an “empty product” or terminal object 1: this is a set with a unique element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in 1
            over any domain.
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of equalizers
            \. For any two functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle f, g: a \rightrightarrows b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi e
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: e \to a
            such that
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f i = g i
              ,
            %li
              Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in a
              over some domain such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f x = g x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' \in e
              over the same domain such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i x' = x
          %p
            An equalizer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi e
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: e \to a
            is again defined up to isomorphism by its collection of generalized elements, denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e :=_i \{x \in a: f(x) = g(x)\} \hookrightarrow a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to c, g: b \to c
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\langle x, y \rangle \in a \times b: f x = g y\}
          %p using the product and equalizer indicated by this notation.
          %p
            Before stating the next axiom, a few important remarks. We recall that a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: a \to b
            is
            %em injective
            if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in a
            over the same domain,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i x = i y
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: a \to b
            as defining a “subset”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in b
            which factor (evidently uniquely) through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %em that
            sense that we say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in b
            also “belongs to” a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            (cf. precept 2). A
            %em relation
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is an injective function or subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \hookrightarrow a \times b
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of power sets
            \. For every set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            there is a choice of power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(b)
            and a relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi b
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi b
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_b \hookrightarrow b \times P(b)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \hookrightarrow a \times b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_r: a \to P(b)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            is obtained up to isomorphism as the pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\langle x, y \rangle \in a \times b: y \in_b \chi_r(x)\}
          %p
            In other words,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle
            belongs to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle y, \chi_r(x) \rangle
            belongs to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_b
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of strong extensionality
            \. For functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g: a \to b,
          %p
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = g
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f x = g x
            for all “ordinary” elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: 1 \to a
          %p
            %strong> Axiom of natural number object
            \. There is a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0: 1 \to \mathbf{N}
            and a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: \mathbf{N} \to \mathbf{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            together with an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: 1 \to a
            and a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: \mathbf{N} \to a
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi h
                  %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  h(0) = x; \qquad h s = f h
          %p
            Or, in elementwise notation,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(n+1) = f h(n)
            for every (generalized) element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbf{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            means
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n)
          %p
            Before stating the last axiom, we formulate a notion of “surjective” function:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
            is
            %em surjective
            if for any two functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g, h: b \to c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = h
            if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g f = h f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is surjective if for every element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y: 1 \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: 1 \to a
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f x = y
          %p
            %strong>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} Axiom of choice
            \. Every surjective function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: a \to b
            admits a section, i.e., a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi b
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: b \to a
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s i = 1_b
          %p
            This completes the list of axioms for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            (provided we pay appropriate attention to our precepts governing membership), and that this theory provides a rigorous foundation for mainstream mathematics.
          %p
            To make good on this claim, further discussion is called for. First, I have not discussed how classical first-order logic is internalized in this setting (which we would need to do justice to a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} comprehension or separation scheme
          %p
            Also, some sort of justification for the claim that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            “founds” mainstream mathematics is called for. Minimally, one should sketch how the reals are constructed, for instance, and one should include enough “definability theory” to make it plausible that almost all constructions in ordinary mathematics find a natural home in ETCS [which can be a definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+mathematics"} ordinary mathematics
            —ed]. What is excluded? Mainly certain parts of set theory, and parts of category theory (ha!) which involve certain parts of set theory, but this is handled by strengthening the theory with more axioms; I particularly have in mind a discussion of the replacement axiom, and perhaps large cardinal axioms. More to come!
          %ul
            %li
              For those who care, we intend to create a page on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
        .revisedby
          %p
